# Opposites — Memory Drill

## Purpose

Drill a Greek word → the word that means its opposite. Antonym pairs give each item a second
retrieval route: the learner who cannot recall μικρός cold can often reach it from μεγάλος.
Both directions of every pair are drilled, so neither member is only ever the prompt.

Target: 3 seconds.

## Item source — NOT hardcoded

Unlike the other blocks drills, `opposites.tsx` has **no literal item list**. Items come from the
database at load time:

```text
opposites.tsx loader
  → getOppositePairsFn()            src/server/fns/opposites.ts
  → getOppositePairs()              src/server/db/queries/opposites.ts
  → vocabularyOpposites table joined to vocabulary for both members
```

The authority for which pairs exist is `src/scripts/seed-data/vocabulary/opposites.ts`
(`OPPOSITE_PAIRS`), seeded by `src/scripts/seed-opposites.ts`. **Edit that file, not this one, to
change the item set.** Do not treat any list reproduced here as the source of truth.

Constraints enforced by the seed layer:

- Both members must already exist in vocabulary seed data as a bare lemma (`greekText`, no article)
- Each lemma appears in at most one pair (unique constraint on the table)
- The seeder normalises `wordAId < wordBId`, so each pair is one canonical row; drill order is
  independent of the order written in the seed file
- A pair whose lemma is missing from vocabulary is skipped with a warning, so the live card count
  can be lower than the seed-file pair count

At time of writing the seed file holds 48 pairs across four bands — adjectives, adverbs, verbs,
nouns — which the loader expands to two cards per pair. The subtitle renders the live count
(`${items.length} cards`), so it never drifts from the data.

## Card construction

Each pair produces two cards, one per direction:

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| `greek` | the **target** word — what the learner types |
| `greeklish` | `greekToPhonetic(target)` — derived, never hand-written |
| `label` | `source Greek — source gloss` |
| `sourceGreek` / `sourceEnglish` | the prompt word, rendered by `forwardPrompt` |

Glosses are cleaned with `cleanGloss`, which strips a leading `I ` so verb entries read
"remember / forget" rather than "I remember / I forget".

## Drill format

**Forward**: custom `forwardPrompt` — small uppercase "opposite of", the source word large in
Greek, the English gloss below in quotes as support. Learner types the opposite in greeklish.

**Reverse**: self-assess. Greek word → recall its opposite, grade yourself.

No paradigm table — there is no pattern to show. Pure paired-associate recall.

Colour theme: terracotta.

## Notes

- Antonym pairing is the whole pedagogy here; do not reorder into "adjectives session, verbs
  session" groups that break a pair across sessions
- Adjectives are seeded in the masculine singular (μεγάλος, not μεγάλη/μεγάλο) — this drill teaches
  the lexical pairing, not agreement; agreement lives in the cases drills
- άσπρο / μαύρο are the neuter colour forms, matching how colours are seeded
- The loader throws when no pairs are returned rather than rendering an empty drill — an empty
  result means the seed has not been run, not that the drill is finished
